findings.evidence:
deepSet walks the key path and creates intermediate maps only when a key is absent, then asserts unconditionally:

    func deepSet(m map[string]any, keys []string, value any) {
        for i := 0; i < len(keys)-1; i++ {
            key := keys[i]
            if _, ok := m[key]; !ok {
                m[key] = make(map[string]any)
            }
            m = m[key].(map[string]any)   // req_resp_decoder.go:945 - panics when m[key] is a string
        }
        m[keys[len(keys)-1]] = value
    }

Reach: ValidateParameter -> decodeStyledParameter -> urlValuesDecoder.DecodeObject (style deepObject, req_resp_decoder.go:699-727) builds props by joining the bracket captures of every query key matching ^param\[ , then makeObject (line 947-970) iterates that map in Go's randomized order and calls deepSet for each entry.

For a parameter 'filter' (in: query, style: deepObject, schema type object), the query string
    ?filter[a]=1&filter[a][b]=2
produces props{ "a": "1", "a\x1Fb": "2" }. If "a" is set first, mobj["a"] holds the string "1"; processing "a\x1Fb" then hits the assertion on line 945 and panics. Because map iteration order is random this fires roughly half the time for two keys, and with n sibling nested keys (?f[a]=1&f[a][b]=1&f[a][c]=1...) the probability rises to n/(n+1) per request.

Nothing upstream rejects the shape: makeObject only rejects values containing the \x1F delimiter, and DecodeObject's props builder accepts any bracket nesting. TestDeepSet in openapi3filter/req_resp_decoder_test.go:228 covers only non-conflicting key paths, so the case is untested.

SchemaError.Error() unconditionally serialises Schema and Value and panics on failure (details are on by default: SchemaErrorDetailsDisabled = false, openapi3/schema.go:34):

    if !SchemaErrorDetailsDisabled {
        ...
        if err := encoder.Encode(err.Schema); err != nil { panic(err) }   // schema.go:3197
        buf.WriteString("\nValue:\n  ")
        if err := encoder.Encode(err.Value); err != nil { panic(err) }    // schema.go:3201
    }

How a non-encodable Value gets there: YamlBodyDecoder is registered by default for application/yaml and application/x-yaml (openapi3filter/req_resp_decoder.go:1412-1413) and decodes into `any`. yaml3 decodes a mapping whose keys are not all strings into map[any]any. Schema.visitJSON has an explicit case for that type but only converts it when every key is a string:

    case map[any]any:
        values := make(map[string]any, len(value))
        for key, v := range value { if k, ok := key.(string); ok { values[k] = v } }
        if len(value) == len(values) { return schema.visitJSONObject(settings, values) }

When a key is not a string the lengths differ, control falls past the switch and past the reflect.Slice branch, and the function returns

    &SchemaError{ Value: value /* map[any]any */, SchemaField: "type", Reason: ... }

(openapi3/schema.go:2023-2049). A body such as `a:\n  1: b` against a schema with an object property `a` reaches this. json.Marshal of map[interface{}]interface{} fails, so Error() panics.

The same panic is reachable for any custom BodyDecoder or ParamDecoder that yields a non-JSON type, and for float64 NaN/Inf via the exported VisitJSONNumber, which - unlike VisitJSON - does not pre-reject non-finite numbers (see the comment at openapi3/schema.go:2396-2401 acknowledging this).

    func (schema *Schema) useJSONSchema2020(settings *schemaValidationSettings, value any) error {
        validator, err := newJSONSchemaValidator(schema, settings)
        if err != nil {
            // Fall back to built-in validator if compilation fails
            return schema.visitJSON(settings, value)   // schema_jsonschema_validator.go:294
        }
        return validator.validate(value)
    }

The fallback is unconditional on the error, so any compile failure fails open rather than closed.

The most likely trigger is ordinary use of $ref. newJSONSchemaValidator serialises the schema with json.Marshal (line 22) and adds it as a single standalone resource under https://example.com/schema.json (lines 43-49). SchemaRef.MarshalYAML emits `{"$ref": "..."}` whenever Ref is set, in preference to the resolved Value (openapi3/refs.go:1189-1197), so a resolved document still serialises its property schemas as `$ref: "#/components/schemas/Foo"`. Those pointers resolve against the added resource, which contains only the sub-schema, so the target does not exist and Compile returns an error - taking the fallback. transformOpenAPIToJSONSchema (line 143) does nothing about $ref.

None of the 2020-12 tests exercise a schema containing $ref (openapi3/schema_jsonschema_validator_test.go, openapi3/issue230_test.go, openapi3/example_jsonschema2020_test.go all build schemas inline), so this path is uncovered.

Note also that the built-in engine used as the fallback silently ignores the 3.1 keywords: visitJSONArray (schema.go:2804) reads only minItems/maxItems/uniqueItems/items, and visitJSONObject (schema.go:2903) reads only min/maxProperties, properties, additionalProperties and required.

