No MaxBytesReader, no configurable limit, and the Options struct (openapi3filter/options.go) offers none.

    // openapi3/loader_uri_reader.go:46
    var DefaultReadFromURI = URIMapCache(ReadFromURIs(ReadFromHTTP(http.DefaultClient), ReadFromFile))

    // lines 51-70 - no allowlist, no timeout, no redirect control, unbounded ReadAll
    func ReadFromHTTP(cl *http.Client) ReadFromURIFunc {
        return func(loader *Loader, location *url.URL) ([]byte, error) {
            req, err := http.NewRequest("GET", location.String(), nil)
            resp, err := cl.Do(req)
            ...
            return io.ReadAll(resp.Body)
        }
    }

    // lines 79-84 - any path the process can open
    func ReadFromFile(loader *Loader, location *url.URL) ([]byte, error) {
        if !is_file(location) { return nil, ErrURINotSupported }
        return os.ReadFile(path.Clean(filepath.FromSlash(location.Path)))
    }

The IsExternalRefsAllowed flag is the only brake, and it is enforced only when no custom reader is installed - the loader's own comments say so at openapi3/loader.go:36-46, 120-126 and 375-381. So a caller that sets ReadFromURIFunc for any reason (a custom base directory, a git-object loader) and delegates to DefaultReadFromURI silently re-enables both primitives regardless of IsExternalRefsAllowed. Note too that path.Clean on the ref does not confine the read to any root: resolvePath (loader.go:344-356) returns componentPath unchanged for absolute paths, and defaultJoin only does path.Join against the base, which happily walks out of it with ../ segments.

