A URL such as "http://host:{port" (unbalanced brace) reaches the first case; "http://host:{port}/" with no matching entry under variables reaches both. Server.Validate does catch these (openapi3/server.go:208-236 checks brace balance and that every declared variable appears in the URL), which is exactly why the router treats them as impossible - but nothing enforces that Validate ran.

    // openapi3filter/req_resp_decoder.go:1682-1684 - the hazard is acknowledged, not mitigated
    // ZipFileBodyDecoder is a body decoder that decodes a zip file body to a string.
    // Use with caution as this implementation may be susceptible to a zip bomb attack.
    func ZipFileBodyDecoder(body io.Reader, header http.Header, schema *openapi3.SchemaRef, encFn EncodingFn) (any, error) {
        buff := bytes.NewBuffer([]byte{})
        size, err := io.Copy(buff, body)          // unbounded
        zr, err := zip.NewReader(bytes.NewReader(buff.Bytes()), size)
        content := make([]byte, 0, bufferSize*len(zr.File))
        for _, f := range zr.File {
            ... for { n, err := rc.Read(buffer); content = append(content, buffer[:n]...) ... }
        }
        return string(content), nil
    }

No io.LimitReader on rc, no ceiling on len(zr.File), no running total against a budget.

